# Project Scaling and Domain Flow Layer

## Purpose

These rules define when and how to introduce an optional domain flow layer as a project grows.

The goal is to prevent business workflows from being mixed into Page Objects, Components, fixtures, or specs.

Do not introduce a domain layer by default.

Use it only when project complexity requires a separate place for reusable cross-page business flows.

---

## Core Test Layers

The default UI test structure should stay simple.

Recommended base layers:

```text
tests/ui      -> test cases and scenario verification
fixtures      -> dependency injection and setup wiring
pages         -> page structure and page-level behavior
components    -> reusable UI blocks and UI behavior
data          -> datasets, builders, generators, and types
```

These layers are enough for small and medium test areas.

Do not add extra layers unless there is a clear scaling need.

---

## Layer Responsibilities

### Tests

Tests should:

- describe the scenario;
- call Page Object, Component, or domain flow methods;
- keep the action under test explicit;
- contain scenario-specific assertions;
- use `test.step` for meaningful user-level phases.

Tests should not:

- contain duplicated long business workflows;
- contain raw selector mechanics;
- hide the main action under test.

---

### Fixtures

Fixtures should:

- wire dependencies;
- provide reusable objects;
- prepare safe setup context.

Fixtures should not:

- hide the action under test;
- execute business flows by default;
- become orchestration layers;
- contain project-specific business scenarios in framework core.

---

### Pages

Page Objects should:

- model page structure;
- expose navigation;
- expose page-level user actions;
- expose meaningful page elements;
- compose components.

Page Objects should not:

- orchestrate long business flows across multiple pages;
- know unrelated pages;
- become domain services;
- contain complex business workflows.

---

### Components

Components should:

- model reusable UI blocks;
- expose UI behavior of that block;
- stay focused on component-level interactions.

Components should not:

- orchestrate business flows;
- know full user journeys;
- access unrelated page areas;
- become domain objects.

---

### Data

Data layer should contain:

- datasets;
- builders;
- generators;
- factories;
- types;
- schemas.

Data layer should not:

- execute UI actions;
- perform business workflows;
- depend on Page Objects or Components.

---

## Optional Domain Flow Layer

For large projects, an optional domain flow layer may be introduced.

Example structure:

```text
domain/
  auth/
    auth.flow.ts
  catalog/
    catalog.flow.ts
  orders/
    order.flow.ts
```

The domain layer groups reusable business flows.

It should orchestrate existing Page Objects, Components, API clients, or data builders.

It must not contain low-level UI logic.

---

## What Domain Flows May Do

Domain flows may:

- coordinate actions across multiple pages;
- reuse Page Objects and Components;
- reuse API clients for setup when appropriate;
- express common business workflows;
- reduce duplicated long scenario sequences;
- keep tests readable when flows are shared across suites.

Example:

```ts
class OrderFlow {
  constructor(
    private catalogPage: CatalogPage,
    private productPage: ProductPage,
    private cartPage: CartPage,
  ) {}

  async addProductToCart(productName: string) {
    await this.catalogPage.open();
    await this.catalogPage.productList.openProduct(productName);
    await this.productPage.addToCart();
  }
}
```

---

## What Domain Flows Must Not Do

Domain flows must not:

- contain selectors;
- use `page.locator` directly;
- replace Page Objects;
- replace Components;
- hide the action under test;
- perform assertions by default;
- become large service hierarchies;
- contain framework-core logic;
- be introduced for one-off scenarios.

Bad:

```ts
class OrderFlow {
  constructor(private page: Page) {}

  async addProductToCart(productName: string) {
    await this.page.locator(".search").fill(productName);
    await this.page.locator(".product-card").first().click();
    await this.page.locator(".add-to-cart").click();
  }
}
```

Why this is bad:

The domain flow contains low-level UI logic and bypasses Page Objects.

This makes the domain layer another Page Object layer instead of a business orchestration layer.

---

## Scaling Triggers

Introduce a domain flow layer only when one or more of these triggers appear:

- multiple test suites share the same business flow;
- the same cross-page workflow is duplicated in many specs;
- Page Objects start calling unrelated Page Objects;
- fixtures start executing business scenarios;
- test bodies become long and repetitive because of shared setup flows;
- cross-page logic becomes difficult to maintain;
- a business workflow needs one stable reusable abstraction.

---

## When Not To Introduce Domain Layer

Do not introduce a domain layer when:

- the behavior belongs to one page;
- the behavior belongs to one component;
- the flow is used by only one test;
- Page Objects and Components already express the scenario clearly;
- the new layer would only rename existing Page Object calls;
- the feature is simple, such as sorting, filtering, opening a modal, or checking page content.

Bad:

```ts
await catalogDomain.applySortingByPrice();
```

Better:

```ts
await catalogPage.sorting.applySort("price,asc");
```

Sorting is component/page behavior, not a domain flow.

---

## Domain Flow Naming

Use names that describe business workflows, not UI mechanics.

Good:

```text
AuthFlow
CheckoutFlow
OrderCreationFlow
ProductPurchaseFlow
```

Bad:

```text
ClickFlow
SortingFlow
ModalFlow
ButtonFlow
```

---

## Assertions in Domain Flows

Domain flows should not perform scenario-specific assertions by default.

Prefer assertions in specs.

Allowed exception:

- very small structural guard checks may be used if required to safely continue the flow;
- such checks must not replace scenario verification in the test.

Good:

```ts
await orderFlow.addProductToCart(product.name);

await expect(cartPage.item(product.name)).toBeVisible();
```

Risky:

```ts
await orderFlow.addProductToCartAndVerify(product.name);
```

---

## Core vs Project Boundary

The domain flow layer is project-specific.

Do not put domain flows into framework core.

Framework core owns reusable mechanisms.

Project layer owns:

- business flows;
- Page Objects;
- Components;
- domain models;
- project-specific data;
- roles;
- users;
- endpoints;
- business assertions.

---

## Main Principle

Start simple.

Use tests, fixtures, pages, components, and data first.

Introduce a domain flow layer only when repeated cross-page business workflows create real maintenance problems.

Domain flows orchestrate business behavior.

Page Objects and Components handle UI mechanics.

Fixtures wire dependencies.

Tests verify scenarios.